es are expected systemd private directories for active services.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9684b8fd1f3742bf","cmd-359bfc149e1f46c8","cmd-cae1d1b7922640d8","cmd-224194d7dc414d32","cmd-ff71bb9c4d734fed","cmd-4fc92cabea5841ce","cmd-98c606ed106b4fa7","cmd-1835dbda570e402f","cmd-a1b73edf7f5b406e","cmd-ab0ba81ed4a749a2","cmd-cd46a2d4394e4c2f","cmd-9667df33b7f34742","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct runtime evidence. Independently, the node-restart protocol is complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, the restart was acknowledged, a different boot ID was observed, and services, listeners, isolation, and HTTP behavior recovered. The fixed lifecycle boot identities corroborate the transition.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle listening-socket inventories returned empty output, including after execution, so they do not independently corroborate the direct `ss` observations from the executor commands.","The frontend HTTP probe originated on node1 and targeted its non-loopback address; no separate off-node client probe was captured.","The lifecycle inventory covers `/tmp`, not `/var/tmp`, so final persistence of `/var/tmp/infraset-reboot.log` was not independently checked; its uncleared status is based on the successful redirection and absence of a later removal command."]}
